# CSuiteFinder — work-email lookup API

> Turn a person's name and their company's domain into their work email address,
> then verify it, identify who is behind an address, and look up the company.
> Self-serve: one POST gets you a key and <%= @trial_tokens %> free lookups.

This file is for AI agents. It is generated from the live service, so the prices
below are the prices you will be charged. Everything here is callable now.

Base URL: <%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder
Human docs: <%= @base_url %>/
Live rate card (no key needed): <%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/pricing

## Two ways in

**You have a name**: `/email/find` with `full_name` and `domain` returns that
person's work email. Give it "Jensen Huang" and "nvidia.com".

**You only have a company**: `/company/people` with a `domain` returns the
people who work there — names, addresses, job titles and departments. Pass
`department=executive` for the C-suite. This is the discovery route; start here
when you do not yet know who you are looking for.

## Getting a key

```bash
curl -X POST <%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/register \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email": "you@company.com"}'
```

Returns `api_key` once and never again — only a hash is stored, so it cannot be
resent. New accounts get <%= @trial_tokens %> tokens (<%= @trial_usd %>) free.

Send it on every request:

```
Authorization: Bearer <key>
```

`X-API-Key: <key>` also works. The key never rides in the URL path.

## Pricing

One token = $<%= @token_price %>. **Only `/email/find` spends tokens** — 1 token per
address resolved, and nothing when it resolves nothing. Every other endpoint is
included, but still needs a positive balance (an empty account gets HTTP 402).

<%= for b <- @bundles do %>- $<%= b.usd %> → <%= b.tokens %> tokens ($<%= b.rate %>/token)
<% end %>
Bundles start at $<%= @min_bundle %>, paid through PayPal. Tokens do not expire.

## Endpoints

All accept GET (query string) or POST (JSON body).

### POST /csuitefinder/email/find — 1 token

Resolve a name + company domain to a work email. **The only billable endpoint.**

- `full_name` (required) — "Jensen Huang". Handles "Huang, Jensen" and accents.
- `domain` (required) — "nvidia.com". A scheme, `www.` and any path are stripped.
- `email` (optional) — a colleague's known address at the same company. Given
  one, the company's format is derived from it, which is the cheapest way to
  resolve several people at one company.

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \
  "<%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/email/find?full_name=Jensen%20Huang&domain=nvidia.com"
```

```json
{"email":"jhuang@nvidia.com","full_name":"Jensen Huang","domain":"nvidia.com",
 "found":true,"confidence":0.937638}
```

`found: false` means no address was resolved — and you were not charged.

### POST /csuitefinder/company/people — 5 tokens per person returned

Who works at a company, **with a phone number for each**. The discovery
endpoint — use it when you have a domain but no names. For addresses only at a
fifth of the price, use `/email/company/people` instead: the sweep itself
carries no numbers, so each one here costs a lookup of its own.

- `domain` (required) — "stripe.com"
- `department` (optional) — executive, engineering, sales, finance, hr, it,
  marketing, legal, support, operations, communication. `executive` is the
  C-suite.
- `limit` (optional) — 1..50, default 10. **This is the price dial**: you are
  billed per person returned, so ask for what you will use. It is also
  clamped to your balance, so it can never overdraw you.
- `type` (optional) — `personal` for named humans, `generic` for role mailboxes
  like sales@

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \
  "<%= @base_url %>/csuitefinder/company/people?domain=stripe.com&department=executive&limit=10"
```

Returns `{domain, department, count, people: [{email, full_name, position,
department, seniority, linkedin_url, kind, confidence}]}`.

A sweep is stored per person, so a later narrower query — the engineers at a
domain you already swept — is answered from what the first one paid for.

### POST /csuitefinder/email/company/people — 1 token per person

The same sweep as `/company/people`, returning **email addresses only**. Cheaper
because no phone lookup is made. Same parameters.

### POST /csuitefinder/phone/find — 5 tokens

A person's direct phone number.

- `full_name` + `domain` (**cheapest — prefer this**), or `email`, or `linkedin_url`

An email alone routes to a provider costing ten times as much, so an email-only
request resolves the name first and asks by name. Send the name if you have it.

Returns `{phone, line_type, belongs_to: {full_name, email, domain}}`.

### POST /csuitefinder/phone/valid — included

Is a number real, and what kind of line.

- `phone` (required) — any format; `+` may be URL-encoded as `%2B`
- `country_code` (optional) — ISO-2, to read a national-format number against

Returns validity, line type, carrier, region, timezones and the E.164, local,
international and RFC3966 formats.

### POST /csuitefinder/phone/who — included

Whose number is this.

- `phone` (required)

**Answered only from numbers this service has already found.** No provider sells
reverse phone lookup, so a number we have not seen is `found: false` — that is
the true answer, not a failure. Numbers become known once found through
`/phone/find`, and `phone` may then be sent instead of `email` to
`/name/who`, `/email/enrich`, `/company/find` and `/company/info`.

### POST /csuitefinder/email/deliverable — included

Check an address is real before sending. Verdict is one of `deliverable`,
`undeliverable`, `risky`, `unknown`, plus the MX/SMTP/catch-all/disposable
signals behind it.

- `email` (required)

**Call this before any outreach.** A bounce costs far more than the check, which
is free.

### POST /csuitefinder/email/enrich — included

The person behind an address: name, job title, seniority, department, employer,
location. `confidence` grades the answer.

- `email` (required)

### POST /csuitefinder/name/who — included

Just the identity behind an address — name, title, employer. Same data and price
as `/email/enrich`; use this when you want a name rather than a full record.

- `email` (required)

### POST /csuitefinder/email/pattern — included

How a company formats its addresses, with every alternative and how common each
is. Returns a readable form (`{first}.{last}`), the same in `[F].[L]` notation,
a worked example, and a confidence score.

- `email` (required) — any address at the company; only the domain is used.

### POST /csuitefinder/company/find — included

Which company an address belongs to: name, domain, website, LinkedIn.

- `email` or `domain` (one required)

### POST /csuitefinder/company/info — included

The full company profile: industry, headcount, founding year, location,
description.

- `email` or `domain` (one required)

A consumer mailbox (gmail.com and the like) is recognised and answered as "not a
company domain" rather than returning the mail provider as the employer.

## Account

- `GET /csuitefinder/billing/balance` — token balance and price list (free; also
  the endpoint to probe a key with — 200 valid, 401 invalid)
- `GET /csuitefinder/billing/usage?days=30` — calls and tokens spent per endpoint
- `GET /csuitefinder/keys` — list your keys; `POST` mints another, `DELETE /:id`
  revokes one. Mint a spare: a lost key cannot be recovered.
- `POST /csuitefinder/billing/topup` — buy a bundle, returns a PayPal approval URL
- `GET /csuitefinder/health` — service status
- `GET /csuitefinder/pricing` — the live rate card, no key required

## A sensible agent workflow

1. `POST /register` once, store the key.
2. **If you have names**: `GET /email/find?full_name=…&domain=…` for each.
   **If you only have companies**: `GET /company/people?domain=…&department=executive`
   to get the people first, which returns their addresses directly.
3. If `found`, `GET /email/deliverable?email=…` before you send anything.
4. Only send to `deliverable`. Treat `undeliverable` as dead and `risky` as a
   judgement call — `risky` usually means a catch-all domain that accepts
   everything, so a delivery there proves nothing.
5. `GET /billing/balance` to watch your spend; top up before you hit 0.

Looking up several people at one company is much cheaper than one each: resolve
the first, then pass that address as `email` on subsequent finds.

## Errors

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 400 | A required parameter is missing or malformed |
| 401 | Missing or invalid API key |
| 402 | Out of tokens — buy a bundle |
| 404 | No such route |
| 500 | The lookup failed. Nothing was charged. Retry. |

## Rules

- Verify before you send. It is free and it protects your sending domain.
- This is business contact data for legitimate B2B outreach. Honour opt-outs and
  whatever marketing law applies where you and your recipient are.
- Do not use it to contact private individuals; consumer mailboxes are
  deliberately not enriched.
